WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.000
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:05.520
So I spent last week in Davos, Switzerland, which most of the time is a fairly standard alpine ski village.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:10.500
There are souvenir shops, babies in sleds, horse restaurant cults and, you know, alps.

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:13.560
But one week a year, Davos is taken over by the World Economic Forum,

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:19.800
a strange and wondrous throng of CEOs, world leaders,  scientists and a lot of security guards

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:22.940
who are ostensibly coming together to improve the state of the world.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.260
Much of the action happens behind closed doors where, like, EU and American diplomats try to hammer out

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:31.980
a compromise over Internet privacy concerns, for instance, or CEOs work out business deals.

00:00:31.980 --> 00:00:36.780
But the whole town is transformed for this week into a fevered pitch for investment and growth and change.

00:00:36.780 --> 00:00:41.760
We're told to "Think India", but also "Think Mexico" and Kazakhstan and particular regions of India

00:00:41.760 --> 00:00:45.580
and also think Salesforce and Microsoft and Accenture and Bank of America.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:47.140
And on and on.

00:00:47.140 --> 00:00:49.680
There was an intensity to the whole thing I've never really felt before.

00:00:49.680 --> 00:00:53.340
It was a week of business and politics and activism smashing together.

00:00:53.340 --> 00:00:56.220
And everyone seemed to feel that they could actually change history.

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:59.940
Like, increase prosperity, achieve gender parity, educate the world.

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:03.740
And of course there's something naive about all that, but as I heard an artist say at one point in Davos,

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:07.480
"Perhaps naivete isn't something to be ashamed of, but something to embrace."

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:12.520
The other thing Davos gets a lot of flak for is its elitism and there was a weird, wealthy provincialism

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:15.920
about some of the people I met. For instance, they kept asking me where I was based,

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:20.400
rather than where I lived, as if the life of a Davos man, and they are overwhelmingly male,

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:24.380
was too international and sophisticated to just, like, live somewhere.

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:28.240
And then when I explained that I lived in Indianapolis, people almost always asked, "Why?"

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:32.420
I resisted the urge to say, "Partly because of people like you, asking questions like that."

00:01:32.420 --> 00:01:35.460
In general, I found the social hierarchies of it all exhausting at times,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:38.520
like of all the ways the billionaire class could have organized their social order,

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:41.460
they seemed to have chosen as a model the American middle school.

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:46.340
And yet, it was wonderful. It was wonderful to see so many people attending concise and clear minded

00:01:46.340 --> 00:01:50.860
discussions of why climate change is happening and how it's going to change human and nonhuman life.

00:01:50.860 --> 00:01:54.380
It was wonderful to see the global goals posted everywhere, reminding us of the commitments

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.860
that we have made to ourselves.

00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:59.480
And it was wonderful to meet with representatives of Save The Children and the UN's Refugee Agency

00:01:59.480 --> 00:02:03.300
to learn about the big challenges of improving the healthcare and education

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:05.180
of the world's most vulnerable people.

00:02:05.180 --> 00:02:09.700
Also, I learned tons about everything from virtual reality movies being made in refugee camps

00:02:09.700 --> 00:02:13.400
to research being done on peer learning, to the perils of economic inequality.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:17.520
And one particular recommendation: After I met the filmmaker Sharmeen Obaid Chinoy at Davos,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:22.040
I watched her Oscar nominated documentary about honor killing in Pakistan and it is one of the most

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:24.280
powerful movies I have ever seen.

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:27.980
Right, but anyway Hank, all week long I kept think about Khurram Hussain who was a father

00:02:27.980 --> 00:02:32.200
living in the Annawadi slum in Mumbai, and at one point in her book, Behind the Beautiful Forevers,

00:02:32.200 --> 00:02:37.520
Katherine Boo reports Hussain as saying, "Everybody in Annawadi talks like this - Oh, I will make my child

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:42.960
a doctor, a lawyer, and he will make us rich. It's vanity, nothing more. Your little boat goes west

00:02:42.960 --> 00:02:47.600
and you congratulate yourself, 'What a navigator I am!' and then the wind blows you east."

00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.260
Again and again at Davos, I found myself up against the ancient question of whether people make history

00:02:52.260 --> 00:02:56.420
or history makes people, and how power and poverty shape our answers.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:02:59.920
Now I know this is easier to say from a privileged position up high in the Alps,

00:02:59.920 --> 00:03:03.840
but I do believe that human progress is possible, even if it isn't inevitable.

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:10.140
And I remain both convinced of and deeply moved by the radical hope that has defined so much human effort

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:11.080
over the millennia.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:15.360
For me, this is actually typified by Sharmeen's movie, which inspired the prime minister of Pakistan

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:22.000
to finally, belatedly denounce honor killing, which is a small but real step toward a better world.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.420
Hank, in the coming weeks, I'm going to look at some of the big problems I left Davos thinking about:

00:03:25.420 --> 00:03:31.060
Income inequality, climate change, the refugee crisis, and the ways that gender inequality affect poverty.

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:35.060
Nerdfighters, let me know in comments if there are other global human challenges you'd like to see us discuss.

00:03:35.060 --> 00:03:39.380
Hank, I have the jet lag, so I'm going to take a nap. I will see you on Friday.

